From: Ian Blenke <ian@blenke.com>
Date: Thu, 16 Jul 2026 11:33:08 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 07213/11362] [outer-loop] REQ-ARC-FCP-5699-32: combined-fix
 live A/B on g50t closes 10-REQ induction sub-thread

Full live A/B with every validated fix combined (CARNOT_ARC_STALL_REFACTOR_LOOP=1,
CARNOT_ARC_INDUCE_MAX_TOKENS=4096, CARNOT_ARC_INDUCE_TIMEOUT=600,
CARNOT_ARC_REFACTOR_STRUCTURE_REMINDER=1), full budget=250 run on g50t.

Structural fix (REQ-31) generalizes completely: all 6 generation attempts
(3 refinement rounds x 2 arms) return proposer_ok=True -- every round
produces syntactically valid code with both required functions. skipped
changed from proposer_failed (couldn't generate valid code) to
no_reachable_plan_after_refinement (valid code every round, ran the full
3-round budget, never accurate enough to accept).

But: NO LEVEL-UP. heldout_accuracy never exceeds 0.125 across any round;
planned=False both arms; levels=0/reached=0. Per ARC Solve Reproducibility
discipline this is explicitly not a level-up -- nothing banked, nothing
headlined.

This is the definitive end-to-end answer for the REQ-23 through -32
sub-thread: the resource-budget hypotheses (data, repair rounds, JSON
validity, token/time budget) were real diagnoses but not the actual
blocker; the interface/structure problem was real and is now fixed
reliably at zero cost; what remains is a genuine model-capability gap in
correctly inferring g50t's dynamics from a small transition sample, which
no prompt-format fix can address.

